            <div xml:id="d493">

                <pb facs="0664" n="544" sameAs="frus:frus1905/pg544"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Minister Powell to the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Port au
                                Prince</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1905-05-11"><hi rendition="#italic">May 11,
                        1905</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1759.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I request instructions in regard to the
                    following passage, for my guidance, in registering and granting passports to
                    Porto Ricans: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p rendition="#flush">* * * is not, within the meaning of the act of
                            Congress of April 12, 1900, a citizen of Porto Rico and as such entitled
                            to the protection of the United States. To bring one within the meaning
                            and intent of that act, he must have been an inhabitant of Porto Rico,
                            residing there April 11, 1899, and must continue to reside therein up to
                            the date of the passage of the act.</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>This extract is from Department’s No. 693 of April 26.</p>
                <p>Am I to understand that a native of Porto Rico, in order to be registered in our
                    legation or to secure a passport, must have resided in Porto Rico on April 11,
                    1899, and continued his residence up to April 12, 1900? Residence before that
                    time, or from birth, does not entitle him to our protection?</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">W. F. Powell.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
